For fun, I tested all char codes 0 - 65536. Besides `\r`, it fails for all characters with codes in the range `55296-57343` (incl.)

E.g. with `57343`:
```typescript
// $ node --test-update-snapshots test.ts
// $ node --test test.ts

import {test} from 'node:test';

test(String.fromCharCode(57343), t => {
	t.assert.snapshot({key: 'value'});
});
```

I have omitted the error for brevity but it is the same error as above

Thanks for reporting this. The issue is that `vm.runInContext()` is used to evaluate the snapshot files, and it replaces characters:

```js
import { createContext, runInContext } from 'node:vm';
const context = { __proto__: null, exports: { __proto__: null } };
const source = 'exports[`\rfoo`] = 1;';

createContext(context);
runInContext(source, context);
console.log(context.exports);
// Logs: [Object: null prototype] { '\nfoo': 1 }
```

I think we just need some additional escaping for the snapshot keys in [this file](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/main/lib/internal/test_runner/snapshot.js).
